Almanac: {
	FlavorText: {
		// Conventional chemistry -- raw minerals.
		Sulfur: Brimstone, scraped off the ash where the world runs hot. Smells like a promise it fully intends to keep.
		Niter: On its own it does nothing at all. Set it beside something that burns and watch it turn polite company into an argument.
		RockSalt: Boil the sea and this is what stays behind. Preserves, scours, and puts out fires nobody asked it to.
		Quicklime: Limestone burnt past caring. Give it water and it gives you heat back, immediately and without discussion.
		BoneAsh: What is left when you ask a bone the last question. Handle it respectfully; it has already been through enough.
		Potash: Wood ash, wetted and dried again. The humblest thing in this book and half the book needs it.
		// Acids.
		OilofVitriol: Green stone roasted until it weeps. It weeps on you too, if you're careless.
		AquaFortis: Strong water. It will take apart every metal you own but one, and it is very smug about the exception.
		SpiritofSalt: Salt persuaded to become a fume. Stays low, finds the corners, and waits there.
		AquaRegia: Two acids that could not manage it alone. Together they dissolve even gold, which ought to tell you something about company.
		BlueVitriol: Copper drowned in vitriol and come back blue. Useful, and prettier than it has any need to be.
		// Alkalis.
		SlakedLime: Quicklime that has already had its argument with water. Calm now. Puts fires out instead of starting them.
		CausticLye: It cleans by unmaking. Do not test which of you it considers dirt.
		FrigorificSalt: Mix these and the bottle pulls the warmth out of your hand. Cold you have to make, rather than find.
		// Distillates and powders.
		SpiritofOil: The first and lightest thing off the still, and the most eager to catch. Keep it away from the lamp, kid.
		Pitch: What sulks in the bottom of the still afterwards. Slow, black, and sticks to whatever it lands on.
		BlackPowder: Six of niter to one of sulfur to one of coal. Get the ratio wrong and it merely smoulders. Get it right and stand further back.
		ColdFire: It lights itself. No spark, no flint, only air, and air is everywhere. Store it under water and think about that.
		// Reagents the book had somehow never listed.
		BottledOil: Thick, black, and patient. Everything it touches becomes easier to set alight.
		BrineBottle: Salt water in a bottle. Not glamorous. Boil it down and it is where half the salt line begins.
		FibrousSlurry: Pulped plant matter. Thickens, binds, and asks for nothing.
		OxidizedCopper: Copper that has been left alone with the weather too long. The green is the interesting part.
		RustedNugget: Iron that has been losing an argument with air for a very long time.
	}
}
